[38;5;231m[49m⏺[39m Noted — refuse by default, overwrite only with [38;5;153m--force[39m. Folding that in:

  [38;5;153mexport[39m gains a [38;5;153m--force[39m flag (default off). Without it, an existing [38;5;153m--out[39m path is an error: [38;5;153mPATH exists; pass --force [39m
  [38;5;153mto overwrite[39m, raised as a [38;5;153mclick.ClickException[39m so it exits non-zero with a clean message rather than a traceback. The
  write uses exclusive-create mode ([38;5;153mopen(path, "x")[39m) rather than an [38;5;153mexists()[39m check followed by a write, so two
  concurrent runs can't both see "absent" and race; [38;5;153m--force[39m switches it to [38;5;153m"w"[39m. A test for each branch — refusal leaves
  the original file's contents intact, [38;5;153m--force[39m replaces them.

  One consequence worth surfacing since this is cron-driven: a re-run for the same day now fails unless the cron line
  carries [38;5;153m--force[39m or the previous file is cleared first. That's the safe default and probably what you want — just don't
  want it to be a surprise at 02:00.

